The Flight Lab code, environments, UI and procedural geometry were authored for
this repository. No game assets, audio recordings or third-party runtime code
are included. QUICKSCOPER and CorpoWars were consulted as QSOL architectural
references; this milestone does not copy their source files. Mathematical
algorithms use conventional vector, quaternion, slab-intersection and rigid-body
integration methods. Development-only tools are not included in the runtime.

Interaction Lab's modules, rules, UI and procedural geometry are original work
for this repository. David Nate Breslauer's Reaction Rumble project description
inspired explaining condition-dependent outcomes; no game source, artwork or
dataset was copied. The symbolic hydrogen/oxygen and neon examples reference
Purdue University and Royal Society of Chemistry educational material. Scientific
facts, scope and source URLs are documented in docs/INTERACTION_LAB.md.

FPS Lab's controller, weapon timers, courses, UI and procedural geometry were
independently authored for this repository. No external game source, artwork,
audio or runtime dependency was copied. Its upright AABB controller uses the
repository's slab-intersection/slide solver, not a capsule or slope solver.

FPS motion extensions, relative-motion queries, stance controls, Low passage
and Tracking range geometry and scenarios are independently authored for this
repository. No external game code or assets are included in these additions.

Platform riding, kinematic movement queries, Clearance lift and Shuttle run
are independently authored extensions. No external game code or assets are used.

RTS routing, navigation grids, the A* implementation, formation destinations,
Canvas 2D presentation and lab traffic rules were independently authored for
this repository. A* and breadth-first traversal are conventional algorithms;
no third-party game source, assets or runtime dependencies were copied.

Shared flow fields, incremental multi-source breadth-first integration, convoy
and evacuation recipes, and search-work measurements are original extensions
authored for this repository. No external game source, assets or runtime code
are included. Multi-source shortest-path integration is a conventional method.

ARCH-OMI-001 credits Brian Thorne's ARCH OMI as its conceptual reference.
The pinned OMI delta law and OMINO tetrahedral incidence notes are identified
in docs/ARCH_OMI.md. The finite-word kernel is independently implemented from
mathematical rules. Four-nibble packing, valued XOR edges, the census, independent
arithmetic oracle and UI are authored for this repository. No donor source files,
proofs or assets are copied, and full OMI protocol conformance is not claimed.

The Brass Engine is an independently authored, fictional mechanical presentation
of the same attributed OMI delta rule. Its SVG drums, cams, staged evaluator,
replay format and browser controls are original work for this repository. No
historical reconstruction, physical analog mechanism or donor assets are claimed.

The Clockwork Vault chamber, six puzzle definitions, graph search, progression,
UI, replay validation and procedural geometry are independently authored for
this repository. It composes the existing game modules and Brass Engine model;
Brian Thorne’s ARCH OMI attribution and source boundaries remain in force.
No third-party game code, artwork, audio or runtime dependency is introduced.

